            <ArticleTitle>High body mass index has a deleterious effect on semen parameters except morphology: results from a large cohort study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1268-73</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.fertnstert.2014.07.1212</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0015-0282(14)01839-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the influence of body mass index (BMI) on semen characteristics.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Cohort study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Single private andrology laboratory.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">All patients (n=10,665) consulting for a semen analysis from October 9, 2010, to October 8, 2011. When analyses were repeated on the same patient, only the first was included.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Recording of self-reported weight and height and of semen analysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">All parameters of standard semen analysis: pH, volume, sperm concentration per mL, total sperm count per ejaculate, motility (%) within 1 hour after ejaculation (overall and progressive), viability (%), and normal sperm morphology (%). Parametric and nonparametric statistical methods were applied, and results are given either with mean±SD, or 10th, 50th, and 90th percentiles.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT(S)" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Semen volume decreased from 3.3±1.6 to 2.7±1.6 mL when BMI increased from normal (20-25 kg/m2) to extreme obesity (&gt;40 kg/m2). The same was true for semen concentration (56.4±54.9 to 39.4±51.0 million/mL), total sperm count (171±170 to 92±95 million), and progressive motility (36.9±16.8% to 34.7±17.1%). The percentage of cases with azoospermia and cryptozoospermia increased from 1.9% to 9.1% and from 4.7% to 15.2%, respectively. The other semen characteristics were not affected. Multivariate models including age and abstinence duration confirmed these results.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION(S)" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In this study, on a large patient sample size, increased BMI was associated with decreased semen quality, affecting volume, concentration, and motility. The percentage of normal forms was not decreased.</AbstractText>
